# Basic quick setup of noggin on Fedora

1. Install and set up FreeIPA

There is a basic quick start guide for setting up FreeIPA here: https://www.freeipa.org/page/Quick_Start_Guide

More comprehensive setup documentation is available from Red Hat here: https://access.redhat.com/documentation/en-us/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html/installing_identity_management/index

2. Install and configure the FreeIPA FAS plugin

First, install the plugin:

$ sudo dnf install freeipa-fas

Next, create a privilege containing the permissions needed to manage stage users:

$ sudo ipa privilege-add "Stage User Managers" --desc "Manage registering users in Noggin"
$ sudo ipa privilege-add-permission "Stage User Managers" --permissions "System: Read Stage Users" --permissions "System: Modify Stage User" --permissions "System: Remove Stage User"

Then, create a role associated with this privilege:

$ sudo ipa role-add "Stage User Managers" --desc "Manage registering users in Noggin"
$ sudo ipa role-add-privilege "Stage User Managers" --privileges "Stage User Managers"

Finally, if your administrators group is called sysadmin, give people in the sysadmin group the role to manage registering users:

$ sudo ipa role-add-member "Stage User Managers" --groups sysadmin

3. Edit /etc/noggin/noggin.cfg to set up noggin settings as appropriate.

As we set up a FreeIPA system earlier, update the FREEIPA_* items to point to your server.

Take a moment to review all the settings in the file and update them as needed.
Most settings in there should have comments documenting what they're for, or are otherwise
obvious for what needs to be set and why.

4. Install Nginx web server and set up web configuration

First, install nginx:

$ sudo dnf install nginx

Edit /etc/nginx/conf.d/noggin.conf to set up web settings as appropriate.

Most of the settings just need to be uncommented to work. However, you may need to tweak based
on whether or not you're using HTTPS and if you are using HTTPS, where your certs are and what your domain(s) are.

5. Optional: Set up Let's Encrypt for HTTPS (skip if you aren't using HTTPS or have your own certs).

$ sudo dnf install certbot
$ certbot --text --email email@example.org \
    --domains accounts.example.org \
    --agree-tos --renew-by-default --manual certonly

Edit the web server configuration file as noted in step 5 to point to your new certificates.

6. Open ports in the firewall as appropriate

$ sudo firewall-cmd --add-service=http
$ sudo firewall-cmd --add-service=https
$ sudo firewall-cmd --runtime-to-permanent

7. Enable and start nginx and noggin services

$ sudo systemctl enable --now noggin.service nginx.service

For more information, take a look at the official Noggin documentation: https://noggin-aaa.readthedocs.io/

